.TH LIBMPQ__UPDATE_SIGN 3 2026-11-12 "The MoPaQ archive library"
.SH NAME
libmpq__update_sign \- sign the current private transaction stage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <libmpq/mpq.h>

int32_t libmpq__update_sign(
        mpq_update_s *update,
        uint32_t signature_type,
        const uint8_t *private_key,
        size_t private_key_size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Generate a signature immediately on a completed private candidate, validate
its structure and preservation requirements, and adopt it as the current
transaction stage. This call neither publishes nor consumes the transaction.
Commit remains the only publication step. Signing should normally be last:
a subsequent actual mutation applies existing signature invalidation rules,
while validated no-ops preserve signatures and stage bytes.
.PP
The type must be exactly LIBMPQ_SIGNATURE_WEAK or LIBMPQ_SIGNATURE_STRONG.
The borrowed key is raw big-endian n || d, using two 64-byte components for
weak signing or two 256-byte components for strong signing. Key length and
representation are validated synchronously. No key pointer or configuration
is retained for commit. Temporary native signing buffers are cleared; the
caller retains ownership of its unchanged key buffer.
.PP
Weak signing uses RSA-512/MD5 and the existing internal 72-byte (signature)
member with reversed signature bytes. Its hash treats that member as zero;
its CRC32, MD5 and FILETIME follow the existing zero-valued conventions.
An absent member is added through identity-preserving reconstruction.
Retained packed payloads and attributes are preserved, including supported
storage policies for regenerated internal members. If necessary, a
signature entry is appended to (listfile), retaining unrelated bytes and
supported storage policy. Unsupported metadata/storage policies are rejected.
.PP
Strong signing uses RSA-2048/SHA-1 and an external 260-byte NGIS trailer.
It copies the archive core unchanged, including any weak signature, and
replaces only a recognized strong trailer. Only the plain archive-range
strong digest variant is generated; filename-dependent variants are not.
.PP
Weak followed by strong retains both signatures. Strong followed by weak
removes the stale strong trailer. Repeating a type replaces that signature;
re-signing is not a byte-preserving no-op. Unrelated trailing data and
unsupported physical patch/delete or contradictory identity metadata are
rejected. Weak reconstruction retains existing full-hash and mapping limits.
.PP
Discoverable embedded archives preserve every prefix byte, the original MPQ
origin and validated user-data relationships. Signing occurs after prefix
assembly. HM3W uses the established prefix-inclusive hash range. KEY
continues to use MPQ-relative offsets.
.PP
V4 weak signing is supported only with raw chunks disabled. The raw policy
is never changed automatically. Plain strong signing supports either raw
policy. MPQE transactions permit eligible weak signing of the plaintext stage
and use existing authenticated publication; strong signing is unsupported.
.PP
Success means generation and structural validation, not independent public-key
verification or external trust. A representable but mathematically inconsistent
private exponent need not be detected. To independently verify the published
archive, call libmpq__archive_verify(3) with the corresponding public key and
require both status == 0 and mismatches == 0. Arbitrary caller keys do not
establish Blizzard trust. Opaque ciphertext preservation does not verify
plaintext.
.PP
Pre-adoption failure preserves the previous valid stage. Qualifying adoption
failures retain the existing abort-only lifecycle. Abort and failed publication
preserve the original source under the transaction guarantees. Private staging
requires temporary disk space for the candidate and, for embedded weak
reconstruction, prefix assembly.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 on success or a negative libmpq error.
.SH ERRORS
Invalid or inactive handles use LIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST. Invalid type, key size or
representation, unsupported signing combinations, malformed metadata and
unrepresentable layouts use LIBMPQ_ERROR_FORMAT. Allocation, size, read,
write, close and publication failures use the existing corresponding errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
